==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|dite}}, {{m|enm|ditee}}, from {{der|en|fro|ditie}} or {{m|fro|dité}}, from {{m|fro|ditier}}, from {{der|en|la|dictāre}} (participle {{m|en|dictatus}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪti/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ditty.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-ditty.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{hmp|en|diddy}} {{q|US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪti|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A short, simple [[verse]] or [[song]].
#: {{ux|en|The Acme mattress '''ditty''' has been stuck in my head all day.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Of Education|passage=religious, martial, or civil '''ditties'''}}
#* {{RQ:Sandys Psalmes|passage=And to the warbling lute soft '''ditties''' sing.}}
# A [[saying]] or [[utterance]], especially one that is short and frequently repeated.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=X|stanza=50|passage=O, too high '''ditty''' for my simple rime.}}
# {{ellipsis of|en|ditty bag}}.

